The Bash, Zsh, and Fish completion scripts the completion plugin prints, in
dist/src/completion/bash.js, zsh.js, and fish.js, are ported from these Cobra files:

- bash_completionsV2.go
- zsh_completions.go
- fish_completions.go

Changes made to the Cobra scripts:

- Every eval is removed. The program is called with an argument array, so a
  typed word reaches it as its characters and pressing Tab never runs it.
- Every construct that expands an operand is removed, Bash's compgen -W
  included. Offered words are filtered by string comparison.
- Quoting is removed without evaluation or expansion: by a string-only decoder
  in the Bash script, written for Bash 3.2, by the (Q) flag in Zsh, and by
  Fish's own tokenizer (commandline -opc and commandline -oct). When the word
  under the cursor holds an unclosed quote, the script offers nothing and does
  not call the program.
- The request carries every word before the cursor and the word under it, cut
  at the cursor, so no empty word is appended afterward.
- A nonzero exit, or an answer whose last line is not ":" followed by decimal
  digits, offers nothing. An error directive offers nothing, file names
  included.
- ActiveHelp handling is removed, including the ActiveHelp environment variable
  on the Fish request.
- The application name enters a script only as data: as a single-quoted string
  in the shell's own quoting, as the suffix of function identifiers in which
  every character outside A-Z, a-z, and 0-9 is encoded by its code point, and
  in the Zsh #compdef line as it is.
- Offered values already carry the option's lead, such as --format=, so no
  script adds a flag prefix, and the Bash script no longer trims the current
  word after "=".
- Bash without the bash-completion package builds its words from COMP_WORDS the
  way _get_comp_words_by_ref -n =: does, and honors directives 8 and 16 with
  compgen -f and compgen -d and a string comparison of file extensions. The
  extension list is quoted when it is passed to _filedir.
- Zsh no longer splits words with ${=...}, passes _describe its arguments as an
  array, calls _files directly rather than through an _arguments action
  string, and quotes each file extension as a pattern with the (b) flag.
- Fish filters by prefix with a string comparison, keeps trailing empty lines
  in the answer, and offers nothing rather than file names after an error.
- Directive numbers are written as literals, and comments are adjusted.
